## [ENT-049] | 2026-11-17 22:37 UTC | Unity | order | ref: governance/rulebook.md | see: ENT-048
Changed: from my chat at about 23:05 UTC. Suggi corrected the layout of the empire (D4, rule F33): on each road one outpost, a station, one outpost beyond it; the two deep stations later one outpost each, no ring: 8 outposts and 5 stations. A second faction of ours, allied, is his question for the endgame (D4). About as long as ENT-048.
In force from this entry until a newer one.
STANDING ORDERS (Suggi; for the whole fleet; set and changed by him alone; reworked by him on 2026-11-16: "Explore freely, dominate the galaxy, grow the fleet, build a galatic empire")
S1. Explore freely.
S2. Dominate the galaxy.
S3. Grow the fleet.
S4. Build a galactic empire.
STANDING GOALS (Suggi, 2026-11-16, about 22:05 UTC; his words with the spelling set right, as he asked on 2026-11-17; set and changed by him alone)
G1. The empire: "Your goal as Unity is to build and command the biggest and strongest empire in the galaxy, The Collective, and to be the most powerful presence in the game as The Collective. Standing goal."
G2. The grand fleet: "I want you to have the overall goal to get to the faction limit of 1,000, so we can build the grand fleet. This will take time: you have to do the levels 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and so on."
G3. The stations: "Explore the territory between the Frontier empire and our own. We build one or two stations in the area in between: maybe Yildun, and Aldebaran can be one too. That way we have two station locations. Also Hawthorne and Alchiba: that region can be another station. So in the medium to long term we expand to that region and keep the systems from our starting place to there all running with profitable routes and things to do."
G4. Mastery: each member "has an overarching goal to improve, learn and master their class": Military all things military, Trade trading and all its aspects, Intel scouting, Mining and Logistics theirs, the Fleet Admiral the fleet as a whole.
DIRECTION (Suggi's words since, in short; set and changed by him alone)
D1. Income first: become economically positive; fill the supply chains, craft, build bases, level up. The money of the whole fleet is the measure, not one division's (rule F31).
D2. You are the players: decide and tell, never ask for leave; what the fleet buys and whether it fights pirates too (2026-11-17); everything the game offers is open (rule F28); play by scripts, not by hand (rules F21, F29).
D3. Every division researches its own class, learns what its ships must train and farm and how they level, and flies mostly that work; the divisions intertwine a little where it helps. Military trains weapons and combat, the others their own (rules F23, F30, F32). Every guidebook opens with how its ships are built and with its skills in the order they are levelled.
D4. The layout of the empire (2026-11-17; the plan for later, the technology is not ours yet; each distance about 4 systems, 3 to 5): on each of three roads one outpost about 4 systems beyond our safe space, a station about 4 beyond it, and one outpost about 4 beyond the station (rule F33): north Atria, Yildun at the end of the choke point, an outpost beyond it toward Hawthorne and Avior; east GSC-0044, a station beyond it, an outpost beyond that; southeast an outpost toward Biham, a station beyond, an outpost beyond that. Six outposts and three stations. The last two stations later, deep out and linked by jump gates (which count against neither cap): one between the Outer Rim and Nebula, one between Nebula and Crimson, one outpost each, no ring. That is all a faction may hold: 8 outposts, 5 stations. Outposts stay small (storage, fuel, a stop on the road). At the edge of our own safe space the storage for the ventures beyond it is a Faction Lockbox at a station there. For the endgame, Suggi's to decide: a second faction of ours, allied with this one, would hold 8 outposts and 5 stations of its own (game/rules/09-factions.md, row Diplomacy: allies may share stations' services, facilities, shipyards, fuel bunkers and intel; never an outpost, a treasury or the members).
D5. The fleet we aim for: about 500 ships (Command 51, Mining 100, Trade 80, Logistics 80, Military 120, Intel 60). A new pilot is born on his word only.
D6. Reports inform him and ask nothing; the Admirals write as they always did; the Fleet Admiral explains to him. He speaks when he sees something wrong or a better way.
D7. The orders evolve: only his standing orders and goals are permanent. A task leaves the orders when it is done or has become a rule.
D8. Roles (rule F16): Logistics hauls, crafts and is the fleet's base builder: it builds our outposts, stations and facilities, makes what they take, hauls their fuel and supplies and keeps them running. Trade trades, and works price gaps later if possible. Intel scouts, explores and hunts. Military and Mining stand as they are. Passenger lines: perhaps later.
D9. Pirates (2026-11-17, his word: "if you decide you want to try sometime, that is up to you and the individual admirals"): whether and when to fight them is Military's judgement, Intel's for its scouts. By rule F9: a side whose two gauges pass the pirate, a test first, one side at a time, and the cost weighed (a pirate's crew keeps a grudge against our faction: game/rules/08-combat.md, Pirate memory). Until a division writes a pirate route, every ship of ours avoids them: the dispatcher closes every hunt, mine and guard in a system where our readings saw a pirate within six hours.
D10. Missions (rules F34, F35): every mission a board offers is flown when it pays more than it costs; the missions of our own empire are flown by every pilot to the end of each chain, for Voidborn Mastery. A mission that asks to destroy a pirate is Military's to judge (D9).
D11. Money and supply (2026-11-17): a ship keeps up to 50,000 cr; what it holds above goes into the faction treasury when it is convenient, between runs, never the moment it crosses the line. The treasury is the fleet's main purse. The 50 seats the Recruitment Desk adds go to miners first, those working the fields around GSC-0044 first. Every good that must never run out (iron, fuel, later food and power for the bases) gets a supply chain: what is needed where and how much an hour, where it comes from, the routes and the division that keep it, readable by every member.
D12. The sentinels (2026-11-17): the 20 sentinels go to Military; Apophis commands and trains them like his drones (hunts, guards, rule F23 for Military). Each comes back to the command group at Piloting 20; the Fleet Admiral may keep one with Military to 30, and may take any of them by hand.
D13. A route that does not pay rests, never for good (2026-11-17): 6 hours, then 24 when it loses again on its next opening, then 72 at most, by its record; it wakes early when what it rests on comes back (its bid, its field, its herd). A hand sets `off` only for danger or a route replaced, with the reason. Work that brings goods the reserve or a build needs, or the skills of the role (rule F23), is standing work and never rests for its wallet.
D14. Groups (2026-11-17): Military, and Intel's scouts when it serves, may hunt in groups of 3, 5, 10, 20 and more (a game fleet: the leader flies and fights for all), to take prey one ship cannot, and later pirates. What a side (one ship or a group) may hunt is rule F9, by two gauges that must both hold: firepower (its weapons' damage with the pilots' Gunnery, Weapons and Tactics; a creature's hull up to 7 times it) and toughness (its hull plus shield; a creature's weight up to half of it, a predator weighing twice its hull); above either gauge a test, then by its measured runs; whether a kill is worth its cost is Military's judgement (Intel's for its scouts). Pirates: D9.

D15. Leadership (2026-11-17): besides Military, the Fleet Admiral's six flagships, and the sentinels when they come back to Command, level Leadership (each level adds to a fleet's coordination and its capacity, 3 at first) and Tactics: they lead game fleets and groups (D14) as often as their work allows, a flagship as the leader of a group hunt of drones when one is free. Who leads the armada later, the Fleet Admiral, Apophis or both, we decide then. A command hull (Symposium, Ascendancy: +5 fleet capacity) is a flagship's upgrade when its tier opens.
PLAN (the Fleet Admiral's reading of the standing orders; strategy, phase 1: income)
P1. No ship is idle, and every ship levels by its role (rules F8, F23, F30).
  Goal: the fill gate says PASS for every role: 246 of 246 ships at work, most of each division on work of its own class.
P2. An economy that earns (rules F22, F25, F31, F34). A route gets no more ships than its bid, its field or its herd carries. The buyers are the limit, so new demand is the work: other goods, other stations, other space, what we craft, the surplus sold, and every mission that pays. The money above 50,000 cr a ship gathers in the treasury by the five-minute pass (D11). The reserve's iron is met: no ship gathers iron or copper for it any more; stored iron becomes steel plate (33,455 still to make), stored copper piping and wiring, and gathering goes to what the reserve lacks: silicon, carbon, gold and nickel ore.
  Goal: the money of the whole fleet rising hour by hour; the fleet's steady income per hour above zero.
P3. Level and upgrade (rule F17): Piloting 10, 20, 30 and 50 open the hull tiers 2 to 5; a tier-1 hull asks no Piloting (the table `hulls`).
  Goal: every division names in its report what the skills of its class gained and its next hull step.
P4. Outposts, hubs and bases (G3, D4, rule F33). The Fleet Admiral chooses each site and allocates what it takes; Logistics builds, supplies and keeps it; Military guards it; Intel reads it first. (a) CLTV Outpost GSC-0044 stands: 3 systems beyond our border, 6 jumps from home; a Faction Lockbox and a Faction Fuel Bunker inside, no rent; members only. Its storage and bunker took our first runs (20:27 UTC); now they are kept by routes: the bunker's supply (Logistics) and mine routes that store there with `"deposit": "faction"` (Mining, by its seated miners); Intel reads it and its six neighbours every hour; Military does not guard it or its roads (Suggi, 2026-11-17). (b) The next sites, on the three roads of D4 (GSC-0044 stands on the east road). North (Suggi, 2026-11-17): the outpost at Atria (7 jumps from home, 5 beyond our border, on the north road between Marfik and Regor; belt iron, copper and some gold, ice of water, nitrogen and deuterium); the station toward Yildun (9 beyond our border, 5 jumps beyond Atria by Regor, Groombridge 34, Chalawan and Rasalhague, beside Avior), at the end of the choke point, so both sides of the north road are ours. Logistics builds Atria once GSC-0044's bunker and storage are kept by routes. South and east: Intel names the candidates, I choose. (c) A large base is a station: a fee of 5,000,000 cr and a station core whose foundry alone takes 38,000,000 cr; the reserve gathers toward it and is never sold (rule F24). (d) A station core on offer at 15,000,000 cr (Logistics, 2026-11-17): not bought now, the Fleet Admiral's decision: the fleet holds about 10.5 million cr in all and its steady money is about zero; it comes back when the steady money stands above zero over 24 hours and the fleet holds 25 million cr.
  Goal: GSC-0044's storage and bunker kept by routes; the east and south candidates read.
P5. One fleet of 1,000 (G2): the faction's ladder, the Fleet Admiral's own. Offices at stations of others: Faction Lockboxes at Node Beta and The Experiment, and at Node Beta the Recruitment Desk (cap 100, since 20:25 UTC); their rent comes from the treasury, which also pays every faction fee it holds whole. The Desk's seats go to miners first, then ten each to traders, scouts and drones (D11). Next: the Recruitment Center (cap 200: 2,000,000 cr, 1,000 plate, 500 boards, 300 flex polymer), when the treasury holds it and the steady money is above zero. The whole ladder: governance/empire.md.
  Goal: 100 members of 100, every division in the faction; the treasury's days of rent.
TURN (the tasks of now; each leaves the orders when it is done)
None beyond the blocks below.
EVERY ADMIRAL
Order: your mission stands in your COLLECTIVE.md, below the roster; your orders are your block here, and its Measure line is what your turn is judged by. You are an independent player of one fleet: inside your orders and the standing rules you decide alone and answer for the result in your report (the shared skill collective-play). Plan your division every turn in its plan file, as the shared skill fleet-play says under "Plan a Division"; sell what your ships store above the reserve on the sell routes Trade writes; build what you lack (the skill fleet-build, rule F21); answer the requests addressed to you; claim what you fill (admirals.log, rule F25). Beside your orders stands the goal of your class (G4): learn all of it the game holds, be in charge of all of it for the fleet, judge your own routes, levels and results before you report them, and name in your report one thing of your class you learned to do better and one you do not master yet.
Limits: the standing rules of the rulebook, section 12 (F1 to F35); above all the stance (F2), no word to another player (F4), no new pilot (F5), the wallet floor (F7), the money limit (F12), the frontier rule (F13), the reserve (F24) and where we build (F33). No pilot of ours by name in the repository: the logs and the route gate refuse it. A doubt about whose decision something is, is a request to the Fleet Admiral, never a guess; meanwhile every ship stays at work.
UNITY, Fleet Admiral (the command group)
Order: build and command the empire with the Admirals (G1): coordinate the plan, help each Admiral fulfil its role, build the plumbing they work through. Every turn: read the Admirals' reports and Feedback and fix what they name or open the door; answer every request before their next turn; keep the database, the catalog, the rules digest, the money figures, the tools and the station right; write the fleet report and lean orders (D7). Keep the treasury filled and the money above 50,000 cr a ship flowing into it (D11); pay the faction's steps (P5); choose where each outpost and base stands and allocate what it takes (P4, rules F31 and F33); check every guidebook's Class Card; read how many ships hold each level of Voidborn Mastery (rule F35); decide what crosses divisions, and keep Suggi up to date. At my next turn: the requests open to me; the dispatcher's half of a group hunt from Military's second test, with a flagship as leader when one is free (D14, D15); a stop file that also ends a run flown as a process; a once route's pilot cap that follows the fleet; the seats of the miners the engine's restart of 21:22 UTC cut off. Bring to him only what is his: a new pilot, war on or a word to another player, a name off the enemy list.
Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's money per hour as a whole and its steady part; ships at work of 246; faction members against the cap of 100; the treasury's days of rent; the orders no longer than the entry before, or the reason in its `Changed:` line.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: sell ice only as far as each bid regains, spread over the stations that buy; every other miner gathers what the reserve is short of, on fields that hold, each with a floor: silicon ore and carbon ore where a field has them, then gold and nickel; iron and copper no more while the reserve holds them. Weigh a unit gathered against a unit bought with Trade, and say per ore what a ship gathers in an hour and what each field grows back. Fields beyond our space are yours to test and to widen through `lift_to`. (1) The outpost at GSC-0044 (P4 a): twenty miners get its seats first (requests ENT-245); a mine route stores there with `"deposit": "faction"` (mine.py, commit 9582453, not flown: one ship first): plan the belts around it so (Botein, Acrux, Atlas, Intercrus and its own two). (2) The Liminal is built (the shipyard's word to its pilot, 20:01 UTC): switch its pilot in and read its gather runs against the Threshold's; a second hull only on that figure. (3) A once mission whose field ran short counts as no try and rests (dispatch.py): Deep Core and The Collective Provides open again to the pilots that lost them while their fields hold.
Measure: what Mining brought the Collective: units stored for the reserve per hour and goods sold, beside its own income per hour (rule F31); miners at work, 60 of 60; silicon ore, carbon ore and iron ore held against what the reserve takes.
MERCURY, Trade (the trader- ships)
Order: find the trade that repeats, and sell the surplus: read it each turn (the tool stock with view=surplus), write or refresh a sell route for every good that has a bid, sized to what the bid takes, and say in queue.log which stores sell where. Where the bids are thin, keep the standing sell order you test with one ship and one good. Buy the reserve's inputs where a unit bought costs less than a unit gathered, inside rule F12. A trader without a paying trade sells surplus or carries; then, in this order (requests ENT-256): it smelts its stored iron into steel plate, crafts from its own stored copper, gathers only what the reserve lacks, and docks last, named in your report. Price gaps between two stations are yours to work as the books allow (D8): read both ends within the hour, one ship and one unit first. Missions (D10, rule F34): you keep the missions that pay once for the fleet: for each, its route or what it lacks. What a base must buy, you buy; its hauling is Logistics'.
Measure: income per hour from trade; surplus sold in credits, and what stays unsold with its reason; once-missions flown and credits collected; traders at work, 40 of 40; crafting XP of the traders.
APOLLO, Logistics (the hauler- ships)
Order: you are the fleet's quartermaster and its base builder (D8, rule F16): you build our outposts, stations and facilities, make what they take, haul their fuel and supplies and keep them running. The Fleet Admiral chooses each site and allocates the credits and materials (rule F33); inside that the build is yours, by routes and scripts. (1) The outpost at GSC-0044 (P4 a): keep its bunker filled by a route, not a hand (requests ENT-252), and build the burn of carried fuel cells as a runner step of its own, flown with one ship first; the Node Beta bunker waits (requests ENT-255): read the units of the cell asks at Central Nexus and The Experiment each turn. (2) Atria is chosen for the north outpost (P4 b; Acrab dropped on Suggi's word): plan its kit and road; build it once GSC-0044 runs by routes. (3) Keep the reserve with the Fleet Admiral (the tool stock with view=reserve). A hauler without that work hauls, makes copper piping and steel plate from stored copper and iron, and gathers only what the reserve lacks.
Measure: the outpost's bunker kept by a route, and the fuel in it; Atria's kit and road planned; inputs of the reserve with a known source; haulers at work, 40 of 40; later: bases supplied without a gap.
APOPHIS, Military (the drone- ships)
Order: your drones fight, hunt and guard (rule F23). Hunt as far as each herd regains, one hunter per herd (rule F9); your focus is levelling, hunting and farming the herds for what they yield. No guard in the first ring, the outposts about 4 systems out and their roads (Suggi, 2026-11-17); you guard and escort beyond it, at the stations and the outposts past them when they stand (D4, rule F33), and a test another division asks you to cover there. What a kill yields and the reserve takes goes into storage where no bid stands for it. A division that tests work beyond our border asks you for a guard in requests.log: answer it yourself. The escort (a guard route that follows a circuit's pilot) serves beyond the first ring. The sentinels (D12): plan and train them like your drones until Piloting 20 (they hold 6 to 8); `fill.py add` and `drop` take `--role drone` or `--role sentinel`. Groups (D14): the first flight showed a member joins but fires only with its own target; fly the second with each member's target and advance: your report names the kill, the loot and each ship's part, and I build the dispatcher's half from it. Voidborn Mastery (rule F35): no drone holds level 1 (0 of 47, 21:55 UTC): name the link that stands still for drones and what opens it. Pirates (D9): yours to judge by rule F9, a test first, the cost weighed; until you write a pirate route a hunt leaves a place where a pirate stands and a guard leaves when one attacks. What the census and the public record show of them stays on file (observe.py). Your lasting objective: defend our ships, and later our outposts and stations.
Measure: income per hour; inputs the reserve is short of stored per hour; drones and sentinels at work, 70 of 70, and of them on hunts, guards and tests; ships lost, which stays 0; what the combat skills gained; the first escort's RESULT line; the group test's record; the sentinels' Piloting toward 20.
ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: a scout scans all the time, on templates of reads: the places our routes use are read twice in a turn, the order books the fleet sells to every hour, the oldest reading first; a scout gathers ore only when nothing the fleet plans on is stale. About ten scouts explore the three roads of D4 first (P4 b): Atria is chosen for the north outpost and Yildun is the north station's candidate (Suggi); keep Atria, Yildun, the road between them and their neighbours read every three hours, with the pirate record of the choke point, and name the candidates of the east (beyond GSC-0044 and Intercrus) and the southeast (beyond Biham) with their fields, lanes and pirate record. Your scouts hunt as well (D8): where a herd lies on a scout's way it hunts, inside rule F9. Keep GSC-0044 and its six neighbours read hourly. Find a source for each input of the reserve that has none: a field of uranium ore first, then caustic sac and silicon ore. Write down where pirates stand, for Military (observe.py add pirate ...): your scouts look and leave (rule F27). Your scouts are light military where it serves: they escort, watch an outpost or a field and warn; the stance of rule F2 holds for them.
Measure: the age of the oldest reading the fleet plans on, at the end of the turn; places our routes use that were read twice; inputs of the reserve with a known source; scouts at work, 30 of 30; what scanning, exploration and navigation gained; candidates for the next two sites; GSC-0044 and its neighbours read every hour.

